---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
---

<Page
  path="/machines/"
  title="Machines | kiln-vm"
  description="kiln-vm creates named VMs from profiles. Each VM keeps its disk across stops, starts and daemon restarts."
>
  <Fragment slot="open">
    <div class="say">
      <div class="say__lead">
        <h1 class="say__title">Machines</h1>
        <pre class="cmd">kiln-vm create dev --profile ubuntu
kiln-vm exec dev -- uname -a
kiln-vm shell dev</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="say__text">
        Every VM has a name, a profile and a disk of its own. Create one and it boots in the background. From then on
        you run commands in it by name.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="form form--lit dev" aria-hidden="true">dev</p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="ground">
    <div class="notes">
      <section class="note">
        <h2 class="note__title">Stopped</h2>
        <pre class="cmd">kiln-vm stop dev
kiln-vm start dev</pre>
        <p>
          Stopping a VM frees its CPU and memory. The disk, the name and the address stay, and the next start boots
          from the same disk.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="note">
        <h2 class="note__title">Deleted</h2>
        <pre class="cmd">kiln-vm delete dev</pre>
        <p>Deleting removes the VM and its disk. The guest gets no shutdown first, so stop it yourself if that matters.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="note note--end">
        <h2 class="note__title">Profiles</h2>
        <p>
          A profile sets the guest image and a default size, from 1 vCPU and 512 MB of memory for alpine to 4 vCPUs
          and 8 GB for ubuntu-docker. You get alpine without <code>--profile</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The others are debian, ubuntu, kali and windows11. ubuntu-docker has Docker set up, and windows11 is prepared
          from your own install media.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</Page>

<style>
  .dev {
    --fit: 2.168;
    --bearing: 0.047em;
  }
</style>
